Title: The Call of the Wild
Credit: Written by
Author: Jack London
Source: Adapted from the novel by Jack London
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action period adventure drama, naturalistic faces, fur, frost, and fabric; 1890s California and Klondike.

FADE IN:

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S PLACE - SANTA CLARA VALLEY - DAY

Sun-kissed lawns, gravelled driveways, tall poplars. A wide cool veranda rings a big house. At the rear: stables, vine-clad cottages, a big cement swimming tank.

(SOUND: birdsong, distant dogs yelping, water lapping the tank)

BUCK, a magnificent hundred-and-forty-pound dog—St. Bernard and Scotch shepherd, thick warm coat, proud head—plunges into the tank, then bounds out shaking spray. He stalks among fox terriers like a king.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck did not read the newspapers, or he would have known that trouble was brewing for every tide-water dog from Puget Sound to San Diego.

Buck escorts two girls along a twilight path, then lies at JUDGE MILLER's feet before a library fire in a later glimpse through open doors—sated aristocrat, muscles hard from hunting.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Men had found a yellow metal in the Northland. They wanted heavy dogs, strong of muscle, with furry coats against the frost.

EXT. ORCHARD - NIGHT

Moon through fruit trees. MANUEL, a gardener's helper, walks with Buck as if on a stroll. Buck trusts him.

(SOUND: gravel underfoot, night insects, a distant train whistle)

They reach a little flag station—COLLEGE PARK. A STRANGER waits. Money chinks between the men.

STRANGER
You might wrap up the goods before you deliver 'm.

Manuel doubles stout rope around Buck's neck under the collar.

MANUEL
Twist it, an' you'll choke 'm plentee.

Buck growls when the rope ends pass to the stranger. The rope tightens. Buck springs; the stranger grapples his throat, throws him, chokes him senseless.

(SOUND: rope cinching, Buck's choked snarl, boots scuffing dirt)

They flag a train and heave him into the baggage car.

INT. BAGGAGE CAR / SAN FRANCISCO SHED - NIGHT

Jolting dark. Buck's eyes open—kidnapped king. He clamps the stranger's hand until choked out again.

(SOUND: locomotive shriek, car clatter, Buck's jaws locking)

In a shed behind a saloon, the kidnapper's hand is bloody-wrapped. SALOON-KEEPER and he file off Buck's brass collar and fling him into a cagelike crate.

KIDNAPPER
All I get is fifty for it—an' I wouldn't do it over for a thousand.

Buck rages through the bars, then lies nursing wrath as the crate begins its passage—wagon, ferry, express car—two days and nights without food or water.

EXT. HIGH-WALLED BACK YARD - SEATTLE - DAY

Four men set the crate down. THE MAN IN THE RED SWEATER—stout, sweater sagging at the neck—signs for it. He brings a hatchet and a club.

(SOUND: hatchet splitting wood, men scrambling onto the wall, Buck snarling)

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Now, you red-eyed devil.

The crate opens. Buck launches one hundred forty pounds of fury. The club checks him mid-air—agonizing clip of teeth. Again and again he charges; again the club smashes him down. Blood from nose, mouth, ears. A final shrewd blow knocks him senseless.

(SOUND: club impacts, Buck's lionlike roar, body hitting packed dirt)

He comes to. The man pats the head he pounded, brings water and raw meat. Buck drinks and eats. Beaten—not broken.

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Well, Buck, my boy, we've had our little ruction. Be a good dog and all 'll go well. Be a bad dog, and I'll whale the stuffin' outa you.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That club was a revelation. A man with a club was a lawgiver—a master to be obeyed, though not necessarily conciliated.

Other dogs arrive and fall under the same dominion. At last a little weazened man—PERRAULT—eyes Buck.

PERRAULT
Sacredam! Dat one dam bully dog! Eh? How moch?

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Three hundred, and a present at that. Seem' it's government money, you ain't got no kick coming, eh, Perrault?

Money passes. Buck and CURLY, a good-natured Newfoundland, are led away.

EXT. DECK OF THE NARWHAL - DAY

Seattle recedes. Cold grows. FRANCOIS, a swarthy French-Canadian half-breed, takes the dogs below with Perrault. In the 'tween-decks: SPITZ, a big snow-white treachery-smiling dog from Spitzbergen; DAVE, gloomy and morose.

(SOUND: propeller throb, hull creak, wind rising)

One morning the propeller stills. On deck Buck's feet sink into white mush. He snorts, sniffs, licks—it bites like fire and is gone.

(SOUND: men laughing uproariously)

His first snow.

EXT. DYEA BEACH - DAY

Nightmare of confusion—primordial camp, wolfish dogs, no peace. Near a log store Curly makes friendly advances to a husky. A flash of teeth rips her face open. Thirty huskies circle silent, licking chops. She goes down; they bury her screaming.

(SOUND: metallic clip of teeth, sudden snarling mass, Curly's agony, then axe and clubs)

Francois swings an axe; men club the pack off. Curly lies limp in bloody snow. Spitz runs out his scarlet tongue, laughing. Buck's hatred fixes—deathless.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So that was the way. No fair play. Once down, that was the end of you.

Francois fastens a harness on Buck. Dignity hurt, Buck is too wise to rebel. He learns "ho," "mush," the swing of the sled. Dave nips his hindquarters; Spitz jerks the traces. Billee and Joe join; then SOL-LEKS, one-eyed and battle-scarred. Buck learns never to approach Sol-leks's blind side.

EXT. NORTHLAND CAMP - NIGHT

Buck driven from the tent with curses and utensils. Frost bites his wounded shoulder. He finds Billee curled in a snug snow hole—another lesson. Buck digs his own and sleeps.

(SOUND: chill wind, distant dog fights, Buck digging, then quiet breathing under snow)

Dawn: he bursts from buried snow in a ferocious snarl—fear of the trap, old as his breed—then sees the white camp and remembers.

FRANCOIS
Wot I say? Dat Buck for sure learn queek as anyt'ing.

Nine in harness, swinging toward Dyea Canon. Day after day: dark starts, fish rations, perpetual hunger. Buck steals a bacon chunk—adaptability, the decay of moral nature under the law of club and fang.

(SOUND: sled runners on ice, whip crack, dogs' breath in cold)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Instincts long dead became alive again. When he pointed his nose at a star and howled long and wolflike, it was his ancestors howling down through him.

EXT. SHORE OF LAKE LE BARGE - NIGHT

Bleak camp on ice. Spitz steals Buck's nest. The beast in Buck roars; they tangle—then pandemonium: starving huskies, four or five score, raid the grub. Clubs fall unheeded. Team-dogs ripped and slashed. Spitz attacks Buck treacherously from the side in the melee.

(SOUND: oaths, club on bone, yelps, capsized grub-box, hunger-mad howling)

They survive—wounded, half the grub gone. War between Buck and Spitz becomes open: Buck shields shirkers, undermines the lead dog's authority, crafty when Francois is not looking.

EXT. FROZEN CREEK - NIGHT

Moonlit chase. A snowshoe rabbit; sixty dogs in cry. Buck leads, blood-lust singing. Spitz cuts across, breaks the rabbit's back. Buck drives into him shoulder to shoulder. The pack rings them—silent, breaths drifting upward.

(SOUND: pack in full cry fading to ghostly calm; fangs clashing; rabbit's dying shriek)

Spitz is practised, cold, untouched while Buck streams blood. Buck feints the old shoulder rush, sweeps low—crunch of Spitz's left foreleg. Then the right. Spitz staggers on three legs. The circle tightens.

(MUSIC: low primal pulse building)

(SOUND: bone break, Spitz's horrible snarl, pack's waiting silence)

Buck springs. Shoulder meets shoulder. Spitz disappears under the dark circle. Buck stands over the kill—the dominant primordial beast.

EXT. TRAIL CAMP - DAWN

Spitz gone. Buck covered in wounds. Francois tries Sol-leks at lead. Buck drives Sol-leks back, stands in the place he has earned. Club raised—Buck circles out of range, wise in clubs, refusing anything but leadership.

(SOUND: camp bustle, Buck's bitter snarl, club thudding where he is not)

PERRAULT
T'row down de club.

Francois obeys. Buck trotted in, laughing triumphantly, takes the head of the team. Record run south—forty miles a day. Then Perrault and Francois pass out of his life. A Scotch half-breed; the heavy mail; Dave dying in the traces, shot behind river timber so he need not be left.

(SOUND: revolver shot far off; bells and whips taking up the trail)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck knew, and every dog knew, what had taken place behind the belt of river trees.

EXT. SKAGUAY STREET / TRAIL - DAY

Worn to one hundred fifteen pounds, footsore. HAL—callow, cartridge-belt bristling—and CHARLES buy the team. MERCEDES, Charles's wife, chatters and weeps. Overloaded sled, whipped weak dogs, chaos. Outside dogs die; Dub shot; Billee axed in the traces. Spring murmurs while skeletons stagger.

(SOUND: whip whistling, Mercedes sobbing, ice straining, Hal's oaths)

They reel into JOHN THORNTON's camp at the mouth of White River. Thornton whittles an axe-handle. Hal clubs Buck, who will not rise—sensing rotten ice ahead.

JOHN THORNTON
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

Hal draws a knife. Thornton knocks it away with the axe-handle, cuts Buck's traces. The sled crawls onto the river—then the bottom drops out. Dogs and humans vanish into a yawning hole.

JOHN THORNTON
You poor devil.

Buck licks his hand.

EXT. THORNTON'S CAMP - DAY

Long spring rest. Skeet the setter washes Buck's wounds; Nig the big black dog shares good nature. Buck learns love—feverish, adoring—for the first time. Thornton takes Buck's head between his hands, shakes him, murmurs love-oaths.

(SOUND: river run, birds, Skeet's soft tongue on fur, Thornton's low laugh)

JOHN THORNTON
God! you can all but speak!

Buck follows at heels, haunted by fear Thornton will pass out of his life. Yet the call sounds deep in the forest. Each time love draws him back to the fire.

At a cliff, a whim—

JOHN THORNTON
Jump, Buck!

Buck leaps; men drag them back from the edge.

Later Buck tears the throat of Black Burton who struck Thornton; later still swims the rapids and tows Thornton from the rock, three ribs broken. At Dawson, in sixty-below cold, Thornton wagers Buck can break out and pull a thousand pounds a hundred yards.

EXT. ELDORADO STREET - DAY

Crowd banked around a sled frozen under twenty sacks of flour. Thornton kneels, cheek to Buck's cheek.

(SOUND: tense murmur, frost creak, then silence)

JOHN THORNTON
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

JOHN THORNTON
Gee! Haw! Now, MUSH!

Buck tightens, swings, breaks the runners free—crackling, snapping—then lunges. Muscles knot under silk fur. The sled inches, gains, steadies. A roar of men. Thornton falls to his knees, cursing Buck softly and lovingly.

(SOUND: runners grating free, crowd exploding into cheers, hats in the air)

EXT. SPRUCE CAMP - DAY

With the wager gold they seek a lost mine through uncharted vastness and find a shallow placer butter-yellow with gold. Buck muses by the fire; the short-legged hairy man of dreams walks with him. The call grows. He returns from the timber to Thornton in a frenzy of affection.

(SOUND: pan washing gravel, night howl far off, Buck's answering whine)

EXT. FOREST TRAIL - DAY

Buck runs with a lean timber wolf—wild brother—side by side among the trees, then turns back alone. He kills a great bull moose over four days of tireless patience, then races home through the brush.

(SOUND: hooves crashing brush, Buck's breath, moose bellow cut short)

EXT. SPRUCE CAMP - DAY

Racing into camp, Buck finds Nig arrow-slain, Hans feathered like a porcupine, Pete dead in his blankets. Yeehats dance in the wreckage. Buck becomes a hurricane—throats opened, panic, Evil Spirit. At the pool's edge he knows Thornton is dead. The last tie broken.

(SOUND: Yeehat chant cut by Buck's roar; screams; then wind and silence at the muddy pool)

(MUSIC: mournful single howl rising)

EXT. SPRUCE CAMP CLEARING - NIGHT

Full moon. A wolf pack pours into the clearing. Buck breaks the boldest necks, holds them at bay until the long lean wild brother sniffs noses. An old wolf howls. Buck howls. He runs with them, side by side with the wild brother, yelping as he runs.

(SOUND: pack feet on frost, necks breaking, joined howls fading into the timber)

EXT. MOONLIT VALLEY - NIGHT

Years later. A great gloriously coated wolf—splash of white down the chest, brown on muzzle—stands in an open space where a yellow stream once leaked from rotted moose-hide sacks. He howls once, long and mournfully.

(SOUND: his howl rolling away; then the many-noted song of the pack)

He is not always alone. In winter he runs at the head of the pack through pale moonlight, leaping gigantic above his fellows, great throat a-bellow—the song of the younger world, which is the song of the pack.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And here may well end the story of Buck.

FADE OUT.

THE END
